  "titleEn": "Windows 7",
  "titleEs": "Windows 7",
  "titleText": {
    "es": "Descargo de responsabilidad: en realidad no he probado todavía la beta. Me cuentan que es bastante agradable y apenas hitleriosa.",
    "en": "Disclaimer: I have not actually tried the beta yet.  I hear it's quite pleasant and hardly Hitler-y at all.",
    "noteEn": "The Spanish archive coins hitleriosa to mirror the official title text's invented adjective Hitler-y; this title-text word does not appear in the comic image."
  },
  "cardDefinitions": [
    {
      "id": "word-pruebo--try-test",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "pruebo",
      "answerEn": "I am trying; I am testing",
      "noteEn": "Pruebo is the first-person present form of probar. Spanish can use this ordinary present form for something the speaker is trying or testing right now when the context makes the timing clear.",
      "example": { "es": "Pruebo el programa nuevo.", "en": "I am trying the new program." },
      "tags": ["word", "probar", "present", "testing", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-beta--software-release",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "beta",
      "answerEn": "beta; pre-release test version",
      "noteEn": "In software, una beta is a version released for testing before the finished release. Spanish commonly uses the feminine article la because versión, the understood noun, is feminine.",
      "example": { "es": "La beta ya está disponible.", "en": "The beta is now available." },
      "tags": ["word", "software", "release", "technology", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-consigo--manage",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "consigo",
      "answerEn": "I manage to; I succeed in",
      "noteEn": "Consigo is an irregular first-person present form of conseguir. Before an unchanged action verb, it means that the speaker succeeds in doing that action; before que, it can introduce a result carried out by someone or something else.",
      "example": { "es": "Consigo abrir la puerta.", "en": "I manage to open the door." },
      "tags": ["word", "conseguir", "achievement", "present", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-que--conseguir-complement",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "que",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; links conseguir to an action by another actor",
      "noteEn": "After conseguir, que introduces the action that the person or thing manages to bring about. English often expresses the same link with ‘get ... to’ rather than translating que as a separate word.",
      "example": { "es": "Consigo que Marta venga.", "en": "I manage to get Marta to come." },
      "tags": ["word", "clause link", "conseguir", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-interfaz--software",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "interfaz",
      "answerEn": "interface; user interface",
      "noteEn": "In computing, la interfaz is the set of controls and displays through which a person interacts with a program or device. Its plural is interfaces.",
      "example": { "es": "La interfaz tiene botones grandes.", "en": "The interface has large buttons." },
      "tags": ["word", "software", "interface", "technology", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-provoca--causes",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "provoca",
      "answerEn": "causes; produces",
      "noteEn": "Provoca is a present form of provocar for one person or thing. Here provocar means to cause a result, not to taunt someone.",
      "example": { "es": "El botón provoca un sonido.", "en": "The button causes a sound." },
      "tags": ["word", "provocar", "cause", "present", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-destello",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "destello",
      "answerEn": "flash; brief gleam of light",
      "noteEn": "",
      "tags": ["word", "light", "noun", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-uh--puzzled-reaction",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "uh",
      "answerEn": "huh; uh (a puzzled reaction)",
      "noteEn": "Uh is an informal written sound showing hesitation, uncertainty, or puzzlement. Its spelling can vary in dialogue.",
      "example": { "es": "Uh... no entiendo.", "en": "Huh... I don't understand." },
      "tags": ["word", "interjection", "reaction", "conversation", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-conseguir-que-subjunctive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "conseguir que + otra persona o cosa + verbo en subjuntivo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone manages to get another person or thing to do something?",
      "answerEn": "Use a matching form of conseguir, then que, then the other actor's action in the special subjunctive form.",
      "noteEn": "The subjunctive is the special verb form Spanish uses here because the action is a result someone is trying to bring about, not a plain fact. For example, ordinary hace changes to haga, and abre changes to abra. Conseguir changes for the person and time, while que stays unchanged. When the same person does both actions, use the action verb in its unchanged dictionary form instead, as in consigo salir.",
      "example": { "es": "Ana consigue que la puerta se abra.", "en": "Ana manages to get the door to open." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "conseguir", "subjunctive", "caused result", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-windows-7-beta-vista-joke",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "beta de Windows 7 · interfaz · Hitler · mejor que Vista",
      "questionEn": "Why does the comparison between the Windows 7 beta and Vista form the punchline?",
      "answerEn": "Windows 7 succeeded Windows Vista, whose reputation was poor enough for the comic to call an absurd, unusable Hitler-only interface an improvement.",
      "noteEn": "The early absurdity builds around a test version that shows Hitler and offers no useful controls. The closing comparison exaggerates contemporary criticism of Vista by treating even that experience as better.",
      "tags": ["concept", "Windows 7", "Windows Vista", "software", "humor"]
    }
  ],
